Application Detection and Response (ADR) extends the Runtime Sensor into the application layer, adding visibility into how your applications execute functions alongside process and operating system telemetry. ADR shows the execution context needed to detect and investigate attack patterns not visible through infrastructure telemetry alone.

When a supported runtime detection occurs, ADR enriches it with application Event Call Stacks and Application context tags that show application functions that would be otherwise undetected or hidden. This context appears directly in the detection details as you investigate existing threats and events in Wiz, helping you:

Understand the application execution path that led to the detection.
Identify the application context in which the detected activity occurred.
Locate the relevant application functions for further investigation.
How it works
The Runtime Sensor observes supported runtime activity.
ADR captures the associated application execution and analyzes the application call stack.
ADR detects meaningful functions within the Event Call Stacks and uses context tags to surface meaningful executions.
The resulting Application Call Stacks and Application context tags (when available) appear in the detection details, providing application-level context for investigation.
Wiz Blue Agent and ADR
Supported languages
ADR application context is currently supported for the following languages:

Runtime	Minimum version
Go	1.21
Python	3.6
Node.js	16.0
Support for additional languages will be added in future releases.

Threat coverage
ADR provides application-level visibility across several runtime Threat categories including:

Application Exploitation – Analyze application execution paths associated with exploitation attempts and suspicious application behavior.
Supply Chain – Provide application context for activity originating from third-party libraries, packages, and application dependencies.
Malware – Detect malware that reuses known open-source offensive tooling, such as red-team frameworks, code injection libraries, or credential-harvesting packages. When these libraries aren't sufficiently obfuscated, they appear directly in the call stack and ADR surfaces them in the detection details.
Prerequisites
The Runtime Sensor must be deployed.
Only the Kubernetes and Linux Runtime Sensors are supported.
Linux kernel version 5.10 or later is required.
Your application must use a supported language.
Investigating ADR detections
To investigate detections with ADR context:

Go to Detections ↗ or Cloud Events ↗ that filter Origin by Wiz Sensor Matched Rules and Context Tag by the Application category to surface detections and events enriched with ADR context.
Select a detection or event to view the Event Call Stack in the details panel. Application context tags describe the events or processes detected. When applicable, you can click a context tag to view the detected function(s) directly in the Process Tree.
Select the relevant process to investigate. Some ADR detections include multiple Event Call Stacks — one per process in the detection's Process Tree. The event process shows the call stack at the point the event occurred. You can also view the parent process, which shows the call stack at the point its child process was executed, so you can trace how the execution chain originated. Event Call Stacks are available only for processes using a supported language.
When applicable, you can click the function button directly from a process in the Process Tree to view the event call stack.

