The Wiz Runtime Sensor captures telemetry from your VMs and containers, giving you visibility into process executions, command-line inputs, DNS queries, and network activity, both internal and external.

This telemetry is structured as Runtime Events, which you can analyze, filter, and export from the Explorer > Runtime Events ↗ page. To search for Threats, learn how to investigate Threats using Runtime Events.

Event collection
Runtime Event collection is enabled by default. To manage it, go to the Settings > Events > Event Management ↗ page.

Once collected, the data is sent to Wiz for analysis in near real-time — every 5 minutes.

Runtime Event collection is separate from Threat detection. If you leave event collection disabled, the Sensor's core detection still works: IOC-based signatures and behavioral Threat detection run independently, so the Detection Engine's coverage is not affected.

How events are organized
Runtime Events give you visibility into what actually ran on your VMs and containers, so you can investigate incidents and Threats independently of the detection engine. Wiz organizes events by the program that produced each one, so you can investigate each program as a whole rather than tracking individual processes. For step-by-step investigations, see Investigate Threats using Runtime Events.

A program is a single executable, such as /bin/bash, running in one place: inside a container, or directly on a host (host activity belongs to a HOST context). Every run of that executable in that location counts as the same program, regardless of command-line arguments it uses. A program that runs over and over therefore appears as one entity you can follow over time, not thousands of separate processes.

What this means when you work with Runtime Events:

Process executions, DNS queries, network connections, and other activity are each reported as individual events. See the event types table for details.
Each event includes the program that produced it, along with its parent and grandparent programs. The event's Process Tree shows this chain, so you can trace what led to the activity.
What you see is a representative set of each program's activity, not every individual event. The next section explains the limits that shape this.

Rate limits and deduplication
A busy machine can generate a huge volume of runtime activity. To keep Runtime Events meaningful and manageable, the Sensor decides what to report on the machine itself, before anything is sent to Wiz. Two mechanisms control this: deduplication and rate limits. Both run on the Sensor, so events they drop never leave the machine.

The Sensor's on-device detection is independent of both mechanisms. When the Sensor detects malicious behavior on a node, it generates a detection and sends it to Wiz separately from the Runtime Events stream.

Deduplication and rate limits apply only to Runtime Events. They never suppress a Sensor detection or its response actions.
When a detection fires, the Sensor reports the Runtime Events behind it, even if a limit would otherwise drop them. This includes not just the triggering event but the surrounding context, such as the program, its execution scope, and related activity. These events are linked to the detection, so you get the full picture and can pivot between events during an investigation.

Event details
Each Runtime Event (also called Runtime Execution Data) carries both cloud context (cluster, namespace, container, host, image) and runtime context (program, execution time, user ID, script name). For the full payload field reference, see the Runtime Event Streaming Schema. This section covers what that reference does not: the event types and their deduplication keys, and the execution scopes that tie events to a session.

Event types
The following event types are supported. The Uniqueness column shows the deduplication key the Sensor uses within a program.

Event	Description	Uniqueness (deduplication key)
DNS query	A DNS query made by the program. Noisy internal queries (.local, .internal, .internal.cloudapp.net, .arpa) are filtered out.	The domain name.
Incoming connection	An inbound connection to the program, with the connection start time.	The remote (source) IP and port.
Outgoing connection	An outbound connection from the program, with the connection start time and counts of successful and failed connections.	The destination IP and port.
Process executed	A process the program executed, with the execution time.	The command line and user (euid).
Socket listening	A socket the program listens on.	The local (listening) address and port.
IMDS query	An instance metadata service (IMDS) query made by the program.	The query URL and IMDS version (v1/v2).
File operation	A file operation by the program, such as access, create, modify, delete, or rename. Reported only when tied to a Sensor alert.	Not applicable.
Registry operation	A Windows registry operation by the program, such as a change to a key, type, or value. Reported only when tied to a Sensor alert.	Not applicable.
Execution scopes
Execution scopes add session-level context to Runtime Events, identifying which session triggered the activity, such as SSH, SSM, or systemd. With an execution scope type or ID, you can trace all events from a single session — useful for understanding what happened during a specific SSH or SSM session.

Learn how to investigate execution scopes in Runtime Events.

Security principles
Data redaction
The Sensor redacts PII before Runtime Event data leaves the machine. This covers everything above, including command lines and process names. Redaction happens on the Sensor itself: sensitive data is never written to persistent storage and never leaves the machine.
For example, a command line containing bash --arg aaa@example.com ... is redacted to bash --arg REDACTED_MAIL<66b38b98> ..., where 66b38b98 is a hash of the original value. This lets you compare redacted values without exposing the underlying data.
Event retention
Runtime Event data is retained for 30 days.
Export Runtime Events
You can access and export Runtime Events in several ways:
Download the currently filtered events from the Explorer > Cloud Events ↗ page by clicking Save as > CSV file. Best for ad-hoc, filtered exports.
Create and export a custom Runtime Events report from the Reports > Save Reports > Runtime Execution Data ↗ page. You can send the report to a storage destination and configure its schedule, scope, and filters. Best for scoped, recurring exports. Learn more about reports.
Stream Runtime Events as newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON) to CSP object storage. Set it up with an Automation Rule, as described in Stream Runtime events to CSP object storage; see the Runtime Event Streaming Schema for the payload field reference. Best for high-volume or continuous export.













Green Agent
Wiz Green Agent proactively analyzes high priority Issues to create a remediation strategy. Critical and high-priority Risk Issues are analyzed as they are detected, and an optimal remediation method is developed. For Posture Issues, the Green Agent provides prioritized remediation steps across grouped Findings. This enables security and cloud teams to accelerate remediation and focus their expertise on the most impactful risk reduction across the environment. You can also manually generate AI remediation steps for any Issue.
The Green Agent automatically initiates an investigation process for the top critical- and high-severity Risk Issues, or high-priority Posture Issues on your tenant.

During the investigation process, the Green Agent identifies the Issue's root cause, suggested assignee, and optimal remediation plan. The Agent mimics a human investigation process, using Wiz data and tools to analyze the Issue and determine the most efficient remediation strategy. The general process follows the steps for Risk Issue remediation, with additional steps for grouping Findings and prioritizing remediation for Posture Issues.

You can use the Green Agent in a Wiz Workflow to retrieve Issue analysis information without triggering a new run.

Automatic analysis with Workflows
Wiz Workflows control which Issues the Green Agent analyzes automatically. The default Green Agent Issue Analysis Workflow triggers the Green Agent on new Risk Issues that don't have a Green Agent analysis yet. The Green Agent must be enabled for this Workflow to be available.

To change which Issues the Green Agent analyzes, go to the Automation > Workflows ↗ page and edit the Workflows that trigger the Green Agent. You can modify the default Workflow or build your own with the Trigger Green Agent step. You can also run the analysis manually on any Issue.

To see the Workflows that can trigger Green Agent analysis and the runs they triggered, go to Settings > Tenant > AI Features ↗ and select the Green Agent widget to open its details drawer. The drawer includes:

Triggering Workflows—The Workflows that reference the Green Agent and can trigger runs, with each Workflow's last run, status, and creator. If no enabled Workflow references the Green Agent, the drawer shows a warning and a Create New Workflow button.
Recent Workflow Runs—A log of the Green Agent runs that Workflows triggered.
Learn more about Workflow run history.

Green Agent output in Workflows
You can use the Green Agent in a Wiz Workflow to Trigger Green Agent to run on a specific Issue or Get Green Agent AI Analysis without triggering a new run.

For both Workflow steps, the Green Agent's analysis results are available as structured output fields that can be used to drive downstream actions. Results of these actions can drive the decision-making process for the next recommended course of action in the workflow. For example, you can automate Issue severity changes by adding an if condition to check the Green Agent's verdict for a severity change, and then trigger a remediation workflow if the severity has increased, or ignore the Issue if the severity has decreased.

Risk Issue remediation
Green Agent investigates critical and high-severity Risk Issues by analyzing the Issue's toxic combination of risk factors with full access to Wiz's Security Graph, raw telemetry, findings, and risk analysis capabilities.

An Issue is created.
The Green Agent initiates the investigation process, analyzing the Issue data (risk factors, toxic combinations, involved resources, attack paths, etc.).
The Green Agent uses tools to retrieve additional Wiz data including:
Resource metadata
Cloud configurations
Identity and access policies
Network exposure
Vulnerability details
Sensitive data context
Ownership information from the Wiz Security Graph
Code to Cloud pipeline
Workload Analyzer
The Green Agent evaluates multiple remediation paths and determines the most efficient strategy to resolve the Issue.
When the investigation process is completed, the Green Agent provides the following:
Green Agent Verdict–A summary of the results can include remediation highlights, but other times the investigation can change Issue severity, or even suggest ignoring the Issue.
Root Cause–The underlying risk factor(s) driving the Issue, breaking down the toxic combination into concrete components (e.g., misconfiguration, vulnerability, over-permissive identity, network exposure).
Suggested Assignee–The results can include the recommended owner(s) to remediate the Issue, based on resource ownership, project mapping, and organizational context. You can automatically Assign to owner within this remediation step.
Remediation Plan–Actionable, step-by-step remediation guidance tailored to your environment. The plan evaluates the most efficient fix for that Issue, and can include configuration changes or code updates. Steps that recommend a code fix have an option to create a VCS issue using the AI Coding Engine.
Investigation summary–The highlights of how the AI agent arrived at its findings, including the data sources used, the tools applied, and the reasoning behind the suggested remediation plan.
Remediation step ordering
The Green Agent addresses the remediation plan by considering the Issue and applies a root-cause-first strategy. The following order represents a common strategy when all components are available:
Assign to owner—When an owner is identified, this step comes first.
Contain—Steps that restrict access or limit exposure, such as tightening firewall rules or isolating a resource. These steps are included only when external exposure is confirmed.
Fix—Steps that address the root cause directly, such as code changes, configuration updates, or patches.
Harden—Long-term recommendations that reduce future risk, such as defense-in-depth controls. These always come last.
Posture Issue remediation
Currently, the Green Agent supports Data, SAST, Cryptography & Post-Quantum Readiness, and Vulnerability Posture Issue types.
Posture Issue remediation applies a strategy by grouping and prioritizing co-occurring Findings. The Green Agent attempts to resolve as many Findings as possible. Some Issues group Findings that share a common remediation or by Findings from a single code author or repository, even though each Finding may require a different fix.

When a Posture Issue contains many Findings with different remediation paths, the Green Agent uses a prioritization method to select the most impactful remediation steps. This improves the Green Agent's analysis efficiency and allows you to gradually clear the backlog, rather than generating a single plan for all Findings at once. In some cases, the Green Agent is able to find a remediation strategy that addresses all Findings in an Issue. In all cases, the Green Agent:

Groups Findings by a primary attribute that varies by issue type—for example, by resource for vulnerability Findings, or by file for secrets Findings.
Sorts the groups by the number of Findings they contain.
Analyzes the groups that cover the most Findings and provide targeted remediation steps.
The Green Agent states the scope of its plan explicitly by reporting the number of Findings it addresses, out of the total number of open Findings in the Issue. After implementing the first set of fixes, you can re-run the Agent to continue clearing any remaining Findings.

Each Green Agent run analyzes one Posture Issue. To work through multiple Posture Issues, run the Agent separately on each one.

How to enable it
To enable the Green Agent in your tenant:

In Wiz, go to Settings > Tenant > AI Features ↗.
Select the Green Agent widget to open its details drawer.
Click Enable at the top of the drawer. This automatically creates an internal service account with read:all permissions. You can hover over GlobalReader to see the full list of permissions granted to the service account. Wiz also adds the default Green Agent Issue Analysis Workflow to your tenant.
To disable the Green Agent, click Disable in the same drawer. Disabling the Green Agent pauses the AI Agent features.

How to use it
Remediation summary
Issues analyzed by the Green Agent include a highlight of the results in the Issue's detail drawer.

The highlight provides a summary of the recommended course of action, which can include one or more of the following:

Remediation summary–This provides a high-level summary of the remediation plan and reason for a recommended course of action.

Issue severity change–Additional Agent analysis can lead to an increase or decrease in severity.

(Risk Issues) Ignore Issue–In some cases, the Green Agent may determine that an Issue does not require remediation based on domain-specific analyzers, like the Workload Analyzer.

(Posture Issues) Remediation scope–The Green Agent reports the number of Findings it addresses, out of the total number of open Findings in this Issue.

Remediation strategy
To review and act on the Green Agent's analysis:

Always review the AI investigation and recommended remediation strategy before you proceed with remediation.

On the Risk Issues ↗ or Posture Issues ↗ page, click an Issue. Its details drawer opens on the right.

Click the Remediation tab in the details drawer:

Top Issues for that day will already have AI-generated remediation steps.
For all other Issues, click Run AI Analysis to manually request AI-generated remediation steps.
(Risk Issues) You can select Choose Manually to request remediation steps based on your inputs. Select an option and click Next Step to build a remediation plan for: how to remediate, the preferred remediation strategy, resources, and remediation method.
Manual Green Agent run times can vary based on the complexity of the Issue. For example, code analysis can take longer than simple configuration checks. You can track the Green Agent's investigation progress in the Activity Log ↗.

Review the results in the Investigation tab and recommended remediation strategy in the Remediation tab.

Automatic remediation actions might appear in your remediation plan:

Assign to owner: Assign the Issue to the suggested assignee.
Send to Coding Agent: When available, you can apply the suggested code fix by creating a pull request directly in your VCS, or create a new issue in your repository.
Want to improve the results? You can re-run agent analysis by clicking the refresh icon to the top right of the remediation steps.

Do you have suggestions for improvement? Click Choose Different Remediation to help us improve.

